Mean of equality_index: .4091335300283518

. 
.         * Verbal description of our model: Event-study regression with year F
> E
. 
.         * Hypothesis 3. Having daughters leads to more gender equality in the
>  household (as measured by behaviors or outcomes).
. 
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear pooled-OLS regression analysis with eq
> uality_index as dependent variable and the survey wave (syear), the employmen
> t status (empl), years of education (educ), years of education squared (educ_
> sq), migration background (migback) and sample region (sampreg), sex (sex), w
> hether a child is still living in the same household (same_hh) and number of 
> kids (no_kids) as independent variables
.         
.         * the identifying assumption is that conditional on giving birth, the
>  gender of the child is exogenous. We believe this assumption is fulfilled, s
> ince it is illegal to tell the parents about the sex of an embryo before abor
> tion can no longer be performed without risking legal punishment in Germany. 
> Furthermore, there is some evidence that female embryos seem to have a higher
>  chance of survival in stressful pregnancies (e.g. Hamoudi & Nobles, 2014) bu
> t this does not seem to meaningfully impact sex ratios at birth in Germany. T
> herefore, we believe the identifying assumption to be fulfilled.
. 
.         xtreg equality_index $event_time $event_daughter_time i.syear i.empl 
> educ educ_sq i.migback i.sampreg i.sex i.no_daughter i.no_son i.same_hh, fe v
> ce(robust)
